{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1791346128277,"sessionID":"ses_eeb7537e0ffe7TnNRtM3LzuDXr","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"XSBMRv7pPl6Mci2r","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/user/auto-psych/src/rsa/ingest/mayn_demberg.py","offset":1,"limit":60},"output":"<path>/home/user/auto-psych/src/rsa/ingest/mayn_demberg.py</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: \"\"\"Mayn & Demberg's reference games (the Franke & Degen 2016 paradigm).\n2: \n3: Three sources, one trial coding:\n4: \n5: * ``mayn_demberg_2026`` — Mayn & Demberg (2026), PLoS One 21(2): e0339899,\n6:   \"Sources of individual variability in a pragmatic reference game\", OSF\n7:   5ab3f (CC-BY 4.0). Shapes and colours, 300 + 6 participants x 66 trials.\n8: * ``mayn_demberg_2023`` — Mayn & Demberg (2023), Open Mind 7: 156-178,\n9:   \"High performance on a pragmatic task may not be the result of successful\n10:   reasoning\", github.com/sashamayn/refgame_stimuli_methods (no licence).\n11:   Four experiments: replication (monsters), remapped message set, all\n12:   messages available, shapes and colours.\n13: * ``mayn_demberg_2022`` — Mayn & Demberg (2022), CogSci 44: 3016-3022,\n14:   \"Individual differences in a pragmatic reference game\",\n15:   github.com/sashamayn/refgame_cogsci22 (no licence). Monsters, a pilot (47)\n16:   and the main study (68).\n17: \n18: Every trial shows three objects, each one value on each of two dimensions\n19: (shape/creature, colour/accessory), and one message naming a feature. Some\n20: features have no message (in the original game: square/robot and blue/scarf),\n21: which is what makes the critical trials solvable. A feature is a 0/1 column\n22: of a fixed six-column vocabulary per stimulus family, in an order under which\n23: the two families correspond (the 2023 paper's Exp. 4 mapping: square = robot,\n24: triangle = green monster, circle = purple monster, blue = scarf, green = red\n25: hat, red = blue hat):\n26: \n27:     shapes:   circle, triangle, square, green, red, blue\n28:     monsters: purple_monster, green_monster, robot, red_hat, blue_hat, scarf\n29: \n30: so the original message set is columns {0, 1, 3, 4} in both.\n31: \n32: Object roles are derived from the features, never from the files' label\n33: columns (``target``/``competitor``/``distractor``, ``targetpos``,\n34: ``answer_which``, ``correct``), which are wrong in places: item 20's target\n35: and distractor labels are swapped in every 2023 experiment and in the 2022\n36: main study, item 51's target and competitor in all of 2022, ``targetpos`` is\n37: wrong for item 20 and for some ambiguous fillers in 2026, and ``answer_which``\n38: is arbitrary when the two identical objects of an ambiguous filler are chosen.\n39: The chosen object is read from ``answer_order`` (its screen position in\n40: ``presentation_order``), checked against ``answer``.\n41: \"\"\"\n42: \n43: from __future__ import annotations\n44: \n45: from collections import Counter\n46: from dataclasses import dataclass\n47: from pathlib import Path\n48: from typing import Dict, List, Optional, Sequence, Set, Tuple\n49: \n50: import pandas as pd\n51: \n52: from src.rsa.ingest.common import Source, Trial\n53: from src.rsa.ingest.fetch import SourceFile\n54: \n55: SHAPES_CODES = (\"ci\", \"tr\", \"sq\", \"gr\", \"re\", \"bl\")\n56: SHAPES_NAMES = (\"circle\", \"triangle\", \"square\", \"green\", \"red\", \"blue\")\n57: MONSTER_CODES = (\"pm\", \"gm\", \"ro\", \"rh\", \"bh\", \"sc\")\n58: MONSTER_NAMES = (\"purple_monster\", \"green_monster\", \"robot\", \"red_hat\", \"blue_hat\", \"scarf\")\n59: FAMILIES = {\"shapes\": (SHAPES_CODES, SHAPES_NAMES), \"monsters\": (MONSTER_CODES, MONSTER_NAMES)}\n60: DIMENSION = (0, 0, 0, 1, 1, 1)  # shape/creature columns, then colour/accessory columns\n\n(Showing lines 1-60 of 498. Use offset=61 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"\"\"\"Mayn & Demberg's reference games (the Franke & Degen 2016 paradigm).\n\nThree sources, one trial coding:\n\n* ``mayn_demberg_2026`` — Mayn & Demberg (2026), PLoS One 21(2): e0339899,\n  \"Sources of individual variability in a pragmatic reference game\", OSF\n  5ab3f (CC-BY 4.0). Shapes and colours, 300 + 6 participants x 66 trials.\n* ``mayn_demberg_2023`` — Mayn & Demberg (2023), Open Mind 7: 156-178,\n  \"High performance on a pragmatic task may not be the result of successful\n  reasoning\", github.com/sashamayn/refgame_stimuli_methods (no licence).\n  Four experiments: replication (monsters), remapped message set, all\n  messages available, shapes and colours.\n* ``mayn_demberg_2022`` — Mayn & Demberg (2022), CogSci 44: 3016-3022,\n  \"Individual differences in a pragmatic reference game\",\n  github.com/sashamayn/refgame_cogsci22 (no licence). Monsters, a pilot (47)\n  and the main study (68).\n\nEvery trial shows three objects, each one value on each of two dimensions\n(shape/creature, colour/accessory), and one message naming a feature. Some\nfeatures have no message (in the original game: square/robot and blue/scarf),","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/user/auto-psych/src/rsa/ingest/mayn_demberg.py","text":"\"\"\"Mayn & Demberg's reference games (the Franke & Degen 2016 paradigm).\n\nThree sources, one trial coding:\n\n* ``mayn_demberg_2026`` — Mayn & Demberg (2026), PLoS One 21(2): e0339899,\n  \"Sources of individual variability in a pragmatic reference game\", OSF\n  5ab3f (CC-BY 4.0). Shapes and colours, 300 + 6 participants x 66 trials.\n* ``mayn_demberg_2023`` — Mayn & Demberg (2023), Open Mind 7: 156-178,\n  \"High performance on a pragmatic task may not be the result of successful\n  reasoning\", github.com/sashamayn/refgame_stimuli_methods (no licence).\n  Four experiments: replication (monsters), remapped message set, all\n  messages available, shapes and colours.\n* ``mayn_demberg_2022`` — Mayn & Demberg (2022), CogSci 44: 3016-3022,\n  \"Individual differences in a pragmatic reference game\",\n  github.com/sashamayn/refgame_cogsci22 (no licence). Monsters, a pilot (47)\n  and the main study (68).\n\nEvery trial shows three objects, each one value on each of two dimensions\n(shape/creature, colour/accessory), and one message naming a feature. Some\nfeatures have no message (in the original game: square/robot and blue/scarf),\nwhich is what makes the critical trials solvable. A feature is a 0/1 column\nof a fixed six-column vocabulary per stimulus family, in an order under which\nthe two families correspond (the 2023 paper's Exp. 4 mapping: square = robot,\ntriangle = green monster, circle = purple monster, blue = scarf, green = red\nhat, red = blue hat):\n\n    shapes:   circle, triangle, square, green, red, blue\n    monsters: purple_monster, green_monster, robot, red_hat, blue_hat, scarf\n\nso the original message set is columns {0, 1, 3, 4} in both.\n\nObject roles are derived from the features, never from the files' label\ncolumns (``target``/``competitor``/``distractor``, ``targetpos``,\n``answer_which``, ``correct``), which are wrong in places: item 20's target\nand distractor labels are swapped in every 2023 experiment and in the 2022\nmain study, item 51's target and competitor in all of 2022, ``targetpos`` is\nwrong for item 20 and for some ambiguous fillers in 2026, and ``answer_which``\nis arbitrary when the two identical objects of an ambiguous filler are chosen.\nThe chosen object is read from ``answer_order`` (its screen position in\n``presentation_order``), checked against ``answer``.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nfrom collections import Counter\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom typing import Dict, List, Optional, Sequence, Set, Tuple\n\nimport pandas as pd\n\nfrom src.rsa.ingest.common import Source, Trial\nfrom src.rsa.ingest.fetch import SourceFile\n\nSHAPES_CODES = (\"ci\", \"tr\", \"sq\", \"gr\", \"re\", \"bl\")\nSHAPES_NAMES = (\"circle\", \"triangle\", \"square\", \"green\", \"red\", \"blue\